	# A layout ON TOP of the game rather than instead of it: the pause menu, the inventory, the
	# dialogue box. Three rows, and each one is deliberately NOT a re-saying of a row that already
	# ships:
	#   Go To Layout REPLACES what is running; these three leave it running underneath.
	#   Spawn Scene Instance adds a copy under THIS node with a bare add_child, which is the reading
	#     it has always had and keeps - a spawned enemy belongs to the layout it was spawned in and
	#     dies with it. A menu must not, so these rows add under the tree root instead.
	#   The scene-flow pack parents its fade overlay to that same root for that same survival reason:
	#     anything that has to outlive the scene beneath it cannot be a child of the scene beneath it.
	#   The named-scenes pack owns the idea of addressing a layout by a short name instead of a path;
	#     nothing here keeps a second registry - the name below is the node's own name under the root,
	#     which is what get_node_or_null then answers with.
	#   Pause The Game is the row these pair with, and the params dialog says so at the moment of
	#     need: a layout added over a paused game only runs if its own root node's Process Mode says
	#     it may. Nothing here writes that mode for the author.
	#   THE NAME IS THE WHOLE PROMISE, which is why the add asks first. `add_child` will not take a
	#     name a sibling already has: it renames the newcomer to `@Node@2`, and the two rows below go
	#     on finding the FIRST one - so a second add under one name used to leave a copy nothing could
	#     ever find or remove, under the tree root, for the life of the process. The add therefore
	#     looks the name up and builds nothing when it is already taken. At most one layout is on top
	#     under one name, which is exactly what the other two rows promise to find.
	#   AND THE REMOVAL TAKES IT OFF THE TREE AT ONCE. `queue_free` frees at the end of the frame, so
	#     a node only queued is still a child, still found by name, and still in the way of the next
	#     add. Taking it out of the tree first is what makes the familiar close-then-open pair work in
	#     one frame; the freeing still happens the safe deferred way.
	descriptors.append(F.act("AddLayoutOnTop", "Add Layout On Top", "var __layout_{uid}: Node = get_tree().root.get_node_or_null({layout_name})\nif __layout_{uid} == null:\n\t__layout_{uid} = (load({path}) as PackedScene).instantiate()\n\t__layout_{uid}.name = {layout_name}\n\tget_tree().root.add_child(__layout_{uid})", "Scene", "Add layout {path} on top as {layout_name}", "Puts a layout over the running game instead of replacing it - a pause menu, an inventory, a dialogue box. The copy is added under the tree root, so it outlives a change of layout underneath it, and it takes the name you give so the two rows below can find it again. A name that is already on top is left alone and nothing is loaded, because two layouts under one name would mean one of them could never be found or removed again.").param("path", "\"res://pause_menu.tscn\"", "Layout", "Layout file to put on top. Its root node keeps its own Process Mode, which is what decides whether it runs while the game is paused.", "scene_path").param("layout_name", "\"PauseMenu\"", "Named", "The name the copy is given under the tree root. Remove Layout On Top and Layout Is On Top find it by this name, so keep all three saying the same one.", "expression"))
	descriptors.append(F.act("RemoveLayoutOnTop", "Remove Layout On Top", "var __layout_{uid}: Node = get_tree().root.get_node_or_null({layout_name})\nif __layout_{uid} != null:\n\tget_tree().root.remove_child(__layout_{uid})\n\t__layout_{uid}.queue_free()", "Scene", "Remove layout {layout_name} from on top", "Takes a layout added by Add Layout On Top back off again. It comes off the tree straight away and is freed the deferred way, so Layout Is On Top answers false in the same frame and an add right after it puts the new one up under the same name. A name nothing is under quietly does nothing, so a close row is safe to run twice.").param("layout_name", "\"PauseMenu\"", "Named", "The name Add Layout On Top gave the copy. A name nothing is under removes nothing.", "expression"))
	descriptors.append(F.cond("LayoutIsOnTop", "Layout Is On Top", "get_tree().root.get_node_or_null({layout_name}) != null", "Scene", "layout {layout_name} is on top", "True while a layout added by Add Layout On Top is still there. Use it to stop a menu opening twice, or to answer the same key differently while one is open.").param("layout_name", "\"PauseMenu\"", "Named", "The name Add Layout On Top gave the copy.", "expression"))
